{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3059","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3059","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3059\",\"parent_revision\":\"b707abc699fa4cb2668c365250373e4da2391590\",\"root_revision\":\"116e1120c77ce4d95f406af30c6802793df09cc610d46735ecef089e4d2f013f\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe address-based navigation path imports the engine's parser and indexer, then reimplements navigation and pagination on top of them. The engine's own navigation and pagination layer has no consumers.\\n\\nOne consequence is already visible: the map drops deep addresses when over budget and documents them as still resolvable, which R2 forbids \u2014 nothing may be dropped or elided to fit a budget.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Navigation and pagination are served by the engine, not reimplemented\\n- [ ] The map paginates rather than dropping addresses; every address is reachable on some page\\n- [ ] Reimplemented navigation code is deleted\\n- [ ] Existing address grammar and response shapes continue to work for current callers\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- Intended behaviour is defined in `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`. Component ownership is defined in `plugins/development-harness/docs/component-architecture.md`. Parent: #3054.\\n\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T04:36:54.131705Z</sub>\\n\\nOwner decisions and verified constraints, 2026-09-20.\\n\\n**Delivery: split. Map pagination lands first, as a child item of #3059.** The address-grammar migration and the deletion of the reimplemented navigation follow separately. Rationale: the map-pagination slice has a verified plan; the grammar migration does not.\\n\\n**The smaller-page request parameter is `page_budget`, in tokens.** It matches `Paginator`'s own `budget` argument and the token vocabulary `head=` and `skip_tokens=` already use.\\n\\n**Approach: the engine paginates, `backlog_core` keeps rendering and addressing.** `_handle_map` sources pagination from `progressive_markdown.pagination.Paginator.paginate_blocks`. It keeps its own `format_map_line` rendering and its dot-ordinal address grammar. Routing MAP through `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator.map()` wholesale is rejected: that renderer emits the `h2.1.2` selector grammar, which `backlog_view`'s own `navigate=` parameter rejects, so it would break this issue's own AC4.\\n\\n**Plan and evidence.** The implementation plan is `.tmp/scratch/reports/20260920-map-pagination-plan.md`. The design brief behind it is `.tmp/scratch/reports/20260920-map-pagination-brief.md`. An independent verification pass over the brief is `.tmp/scratch/reports/20260920-map-pagination-claim-check.md`. The brief carries an in-place correction where its claims did not survive checking; read the claim-check table before relying on any figure in it.\\n\\n**Constraints established by measurement, not by the brief:**\\n\\n- Schema headroom is tighter than the plan assumes. `backlog_view`'s output schema measures 1,706 tokens against its own 2,000 ceiling, leaving 294. The total across all tools measures 12,773 against the 13,000 ceiling in `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_tool_output_schemas.py`, leaving 227. The total is the binding constraint on `page`, `total_pages` and `page_budget` plus their descriptions.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_tool_output_schemas.py:44` says the schema measures 1,904 tokens. Three independent measurements say 1,706. Correct that comment as part of this work.\\n- `BacklogViewResponse` re-declares the map fields at `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tool_responses.py:994-997`. A field added only to `MapResponse` is dropped at the wire boundary with no test failure. `next_call` already exists at `:1005` and can be reused, so only `page` and `total_pages` are new there.\\n- `Paginator.paginate_blocks` joins blocks with the empty string (`progressive_markdown/pagination.py:79`, `:87`) and `format_map_line` emits no trailing newline. Blocks must carry their own newline, and the single-page case must strip one to stay byte-identical.\\n\\n**#3190 does not block this work.** The brief cited it as engine-level addressing instability. It is not. #3190's description cites `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_content_normalizer.py:362-376`, which is `backlog_core`'s own normalizer path \u2014 the layer this approach keeps. #3190 is `needs-grooming` and its \\\"Not Established\\\" section holds both of its premises open, including whether any write path can produce a duplicate heading at all.\\n\\n**Callers that describe MAP output as complete, and must change with it:** `plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md`, `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md`, and `backlog_view`'s own MCP tool docstring in `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py`. A fourth document asserts the unbounded-map behaviour: `.claude/agent-memory/dh-service-docs-maintainer/unenforced-map-guarantee.md`.\\n\\n**Removing #3060's warning sentence is an acceptance criterion of this work.** `_build_over_budget_view`'s `_usage` text currently says the map is \\\"usually small enough\\\". That sentence describes the condition this change removes.\\n\\n**Separate item, not fixed here:** `TokenBudgeter.__init__`'s `default_budget=11000` (`progressive_markdown/tokenizer.py:32`) disagrees with `_DEFAULT_BUDGET` and would trap a future caller that constructs `TokenBudgeter()` directly. No current code path reaches it.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Verified Evidence The Map Is Unbounded Not Merely Lossy\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe map handler in `disclosure_handler.py` assembles its output as:\\n\\n```python\\nmap_text = \\\"\\\\n\\\".join(mapper.format_map_line(e) for e in entries)\\n```\\n\\nEvery entry, joined. No budget check, no truncation, no pagination. An `over_budget` boolean is computed alongside and the full text is returned regardless.\\n\\nThree separate places assert a bound that does not exist:\\n\\n- the map response type's own docstring states the text is always under roughly 2,000 tokens regardless of item size\\n- `docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md` described the text as bounded by the token budget, with deep addresses absent but still resolvable\\n- the agent-facing documentation added in PR #3051 repeated the bound as a guarantee (being corrected on that PR)\\n\\nSo the earlier characterisation of this as an invented-limit problem was wrong in the safer direction: nothing is dropped because nothing is bounded. A map for a large item exceeds a harness response cap outright rather than degrading.\\n\\nAdds to this item's acceptance criteria: the map response is bounded and paginated, and no docstring or document asserts a bound the implementation does not enforce.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Owner Decisions\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T04:32:52.039021Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-09-20T04:41:40.310814Z \u2014 Duplicate. The same decisions are recorded in the Scope section under entry 2026-09-20T04:36:54.131705Z, using a registered section name. This copy is superseded, not retracted on its merits.</summary>\\n\\nOwner decisions, 2026-09-20.\\n\\n**Delivery: split. Map pagination lands first, as a child item of #3059.** The address-grammar migration and the deletion of the reimplemented navigation follow separately. Rationale: the map-pagination slice has a verified plan; the grammar migration does not.\\n\\n**The smaller-page request parameter is `page_budget`, in tokens.** It matches `Paginator`'s own `budget` argument and the token vocabulary `head=` and `skip_tokens=` already use.\\n\\n**Approach: the engine paginates, `backlog_core` keeps rendering and addressing.** `_handle_map` sources pagination from `progressive_markdown.pagination.Paginator.paginate_blocks`. It keeps its own `format_map_line` rendering and its dot-ordinal address grammar. Routing MAP through `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator.map()` wholesale is rejected: that renderer emits the `h2.1.2` selector grammar, which `backlog_view`'s own `navigate=` parameter rejects, so it would break this issue's own AC4.\\n\\n**Plan and evidence.** The implementation plan is `.tmp/scratch/reports/20260920-map-pagination-plan.md`. The design brief behind it is `.tmp/scratch/reports/20260920-map-pagination-brief.md`. An independent verification pass over the brief is `.tmp/scratch/reports/20260920-map-pagination-claim-check.md`. The brief carries an in-place correction where its claims did not survive checking; read the claim-check table before relying on any figure in it.\\n\\n**Constraints established by measurement, not by the brief:**\\n\\n- Schema headroom is tighter than the plan assumes. `backlog_view`'s output schema measures 1,706 tokens against its own 2,000 ceiling, leaving 294. The total across all tools measures 12,773 against the 13,000 ceiling in `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_tool_output_schemas.py`, leaving **227**. The total is the binding constraint on `page`, `total_pages` and `page_budget` plus their descriptions.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_tool_output_schemas.py:44` says the schema measures 1,904 tokens. Three independent measurements say 1,706. Correct that comment as part of this work.\\n- `BacklogViewResponse` re-declares the map fields at `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tool_responses.py:994-997`. A field added only to `MapResponse` is dropped at the wire boundary with no test failure. `next_call` already exists at `:1005` and can be reused, so only `page` and `total_pages` are new there.\\n- `Paginator.paginate_blocks` joins bloc